The buffered-channel example compiles and executes its definitions
examples/channels.links
filemode : true
stdout : () : ()

Buffered channels retain FIFO order for two writes
open import Channels; var c=(newChannel() : Channel(Int)); { put(c,40); put(c,42); (get(c),get(c)) }
stdout : (40, 42) : (Int, Int)

A waiting channel reader resumes after a later write
tests/fresco_examples/channels-waiting.links
filemode : true
stdout : 42 : Int

Channels carry strings and supplied absolute function messages
tests/fresco_examples/channels-payloads.links
filemode : true
stdout : ("first", "second", 42) : (String, String, Int)

Channel helpers preserve contextual payloads and the alias accepts linear results
tests/fresco_examples/channels-helpers.links
filemode : true
stdout : 42 : Int

Channel construction requires Wild
open import Channels; sig pure : () -> Channel(Int) fun pure() { newChannel() } ()
exit : 1
stderr : @.*Type error: Type does not match the required type.*In expression: newChannel\.

Reading a channel requires Wild
open import Channels; sig pure : (Channel(Int)) -> Int fun pure(c) { get(c) } ()
exit : 1
stderr : @.*Type error: Type does not match the required type.*In expression: get\.

Writing a channel requires Wild
open import Channels; sig pure : (Channel(Int),Int) -> () fun pure(c,x) { put(c,x) } ()
exit : 1
stderr : @.*Type error: Type does not match the required type.*In expression: put\.

Queued channel messages require absolute payloads
open import Channels; effect E { Read : () => Int; } typename F = () -> Int; handle <E> ({ var f=fun() { do Read }; var c= ~newChannel[F](); put(c,f); 42 }) { case x -> x case <Read => k> -> k(42) }
exit : 1
stderr : @.*Type error: Type does not satisfy its kind or usage requirement.*In expression: ~newChannel\[F\]\.

Queued channel messages retain their unrestricted ordinary kind
open import Channels; ~newChannel[End]()
exit : 1
stderr : @.*Type error: Type does not satisfy its kind or usage requirement.*In expression: ~newChannel\[End\]\.

The identity handler retains polymorphism and caller effects
open import Identity; effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } var i=force(fun(){42}); var b=force(fun(){true}); var r=handle (force(fun(){do Ask})) { case x -> x case <Ask => k> -> k(7) }; (i,b,r)
stdout : (42, true, 7) : (Int, Bool, Int)
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The number game retains its original messages and execution
open import Number_games; example()
stdout : @Take a guess> 64\nYour guess is too high\.\nTake a guess> 32\nYour guess is too low\.\nTake a guess> 48\nYour guess is too high\.\nTake a guess> 40\nYour guess is too low\.\nTake a guess> 44\nYour guess is too high\.\nTake a guess> 42\nCorrect!!\n\(\) : \(\)$
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The logged number game retains its messages and reverse log order
open import Number_games; example2()
stdout : @Take a guess> 64\nYour guess is too high\.\nTake a guess> 32\nYour guess is too low\.\nTake a guess> 48\nYour guess is too high\.\nTake a guess> 40\nYour guess is too low\.\nTake a guess> 44\nYour guess is too high\.\nTake a guess> 42\nCorrect!!\n\[\(42, Correct\), \(44, High\), \(40, Low\), \(48, High\), \(32, Low\), \(64, High\)\] : List \(Int, Number_games\.Answer\)$
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The secret handler retains different result types
open import Number_games; (mySecret(42,fun(){var _=do Guess(42); 7}),mySecret(42,fun(){var _=do Guess(42); "done"}),input([],fun(){true}),isNil([1]),isNil([false]))
stdout : (7, "done", (), false, false) : (Int, String, (), Bool, Bool)
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The secret handler returns contextual functions using residual effects
open import Number_games; effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } handle ({var action=mySecret(42,fun(){var _=do Guess(42); fun(){do Ask}}); action()}) {case x -> x case <Ask => k> -> k(7)}
stdout : 7 : Int
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The guess logger forwards guesses and retains caller effects
open import Number_games; effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } handle (mySecret(42,fun(){logger(fun(){var _=do Guess(64); var _=do Ask; var _=do Guess(32); var _=do Guess(42); ()})})) {case xs -> xs case <Ask => k> -> print("read "); k(7)}
stdout : read [(42, Correct), (32, Low), (64, High)] : List (Int, [|Correct|High|Low|])
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The guess logger requires an outer guess handler
open import Number_games; logger(fun(){var _=do Guess(42); ()})
exit : 1
stderr : @.*Type error:.*In expression: logger\.
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The identity handler retains linear results
open import Identity; var k=force(fun(){linfun(){42}}); k()
stdout : 42 : Int
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The U2 puzzle retains both complete solutions in order
open import FrescoPrelude; open import U2_puzzle; var sols=solutions(); (length(sols),sols)
stdout : (2, [[([Bono, Edge], true), ([Bono], false), ([Adam, Larry], true), ([Edge], false), ([Bono, Edge], true)], [([Bono, Edge], true), ([Edge], false), ([Adam, Larry], true), ([Bono], false), ([Bono, Edge], true)]]) : (Int, List (List (List (U2_puzzle.U2), Bool)))
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

The U2 puzzle retains printed parties and directions
open import FrescoPrelude; open import U2_puzzle; printSolutions(solutions())
stdout : @=== Solution ===\nforward: Bono, Edge\nback: Bono\nforward: Adam, Larry\nback: Edge\nforward: Bono, Edge\n=== Solution ===\nforward: Bono, Edge\nback: Edge\nforward: Adam, Larry\nback: Bono\nforward: Bono, Edge\n\(\) : \(\)$
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

U2 search choice retains generic integer payloads
open import FrescoPrelude; open import U2_puzzle; handle <Search(Int)> (choose([42,7])) {case x -> [x] case <Choose(xs) => k> -> concatMap(k,xs) case <Fail> -> []}
stdout : [42, 7] : List Int
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

U2 search choice retains generic boolean payloads
open import FrescoPrelude; open import U2_puzzle; handle <Search(Bool)> (choose([true,false])) {case x -> [x] case <Choose(xs) => k> -> concatMap(k,xs) case <Fail> -> []}
stdout : [true, false] : List Bool
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

U2 search failure retains results independent of choice payloads
open import FrescoPrelude; open import U2_puzzle; var b=handle <Search(Int)> (~fail[Int,Bool]()) {case x -> x case <Choose(xs) => k> -> k(hd(xs)) case <Fail> -> true}; var s=handle <Search(Bool)> (~fail[Bool,String]()) {case x -> x case <Choose(xs) => k> -> k(hd(xs)) case <Fail> -> "failed"}; (b,s)
stdout : (true, "failed") : (Bool, String)
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

U2 search failure retains contextual result types
open import FrescoPrelude; open import U2_puzzle; effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } handle ({var f=handle <Search(Int)> (~fail[Int,(() -> Int)]()) {case _ -> fun(){0} case <Choose(xs) => k> -> k(hd(xs)) case <Fail> -> fun(){do Ask}}; f()}) {case x -> x case <Ask => k> -> k(7)}
stdout : 7 : Int
args : --fresco --path=examples/handlers:examples:lib/stdlib

